WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.842
For a small number of category mappings, a case expression is easy to read and teach.

00:00:06.842 --> 00:00:10.947
The query first handles missing or blank categories.

00:00:10.947 --> 00:00:20.712
Then it converts the source text to a consistent form and checks whether it belongs to one of several known groups.

00:00:20.712 --> 00:00:26.837
For example, electronics, electronic, and tech are mapped to Electronics.

00:00:26.837 --> 00:00:30.512
Audio and headphones are mapped to Audio.

00:00:30.512 --> 00:00:35.045
Other known labels are handled in the same way.

00:00:35.696 --> 00:00:43.383
If a value does not match one of the documented groups, the query keeps it but gives it consistent capitalization.

00:00:43.383 --> 00:00:47.323
This approach works well for a small teaching example.

00:00:47.323 --> 00:00:54.293
In a production system with many mappings, a separate lookup table is usually easier to maintain.

00:00:54.293 --> 00:00:59.408
Run the query and compare the raw category with the standardized category.

00:00:59.408 --> 00:01:03.686
Focus on the idea that the mapping rule is explicit and reviewable.
